1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:01,940
On November 23, as US markets opened

2
00:00:02,380 --> 00:00:04,500
Nvidia's stock jumped 30%

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:05,970
Not because of a new chip

4
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:07,460
and not because earnings beat expectations

5
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:09,520
but because someone who had just resigned

6
00:00:09,990 --> 00:00:11,600
was invited back by the board

7
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:13,100
On November 12

8
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:15,720
Jensen Huang stepped down as Nvidia's CEO and director

9
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:18,760
CFO Colette Kress took over in the interim

10
00:00:19,190 --> 00:00:20,680
For the first time in the company's 33 years

11
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:22,500
his name wasn't on it

12
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:25,560
That week, Nvidia's stock plunged 14%

13
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:27,540
and signatures on a petition for his return

14
00:00:28,070 --> 00:00:30,000
grew from about 110,000 to 180,000

15
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,620
Several thousand of them said they were Nvidia employees

16
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:35,400
Even stranger was the November 17 earnings report

17
00:00:35,830 --> 00:00:37,480
Revenue of $115.8 billion

18
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:39,500
and net profit of $65.3 billion

19
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:40,900
Not a single order was lost

20
00:00:41,290 --> 00:00:43,860
Yet that same day, the board said it had no plans to reinstate him

21
00:00:44,310 --> 00:00:45,280
Most people's first reaction is

22
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,060
isn't this just the founder's halo, a sentimental drama

23
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:50,400
What does this have to do with economics

24
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:51,710
But the market

25
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:54,400
doesn't hand out a 30% jump for sentiment

26
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:55,570
What it's really doing

27
00:00:55,620 --> 00:00:57,900
is pricing something that never appears on the financial statements

28
00:00:58,290 --> 00:00:59,320
This is Pixel Breath

29
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:01,110
Today, let's talk about

30
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:03,080
why a company can't do without one person

31
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,880
Let's start by laying out the timeline

32
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:08,140
November 12, Jensen Huang resigns

33
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:09,890
Kress steps in as interim CEO

34
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:12,020
Harvey Jones, a former CEO of another company

35
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:14,320
becomes the company's first-ever chairman

36
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:16,760
The company said its roadmap won't change

37
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:18,510
and its contracts won't be broken

38
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:20,430
Honestly, I used to think the same way

39
00:01:20,830 --> 00:01:22,820
that the world's most valuable chip company

40
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:25,690
must rely on its products and customer loyalty

41
00:01:26,290 --> 00:01:28,450
and its patents, not on any one person

42
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:31,790
and that systems should be more reliable than one person's reputation

43
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:34,190
But the market had no time to wait

44
00:01:34,630 --> 00:01:36,710
That week, the stock plunged 14%

45
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:38,580
and signatures asking Jensen Huang to return

46
00:01:38,630 --> 00:01:40,510
rose from 110,000 to 180,000

47
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:41,960
Before the earnings were even out

48
00:01:42,010 --> 00:01:44,430
people had already voted with their feet

49
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:46,330
When the earnings came out on November 17

50
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:47,550
the numbers were rock solid

51
00:01:48,010 --> 00:01:49,970
Total revenue was $115.8 billion

52
00:01:50,410 --> 00:01:52,320
up 103% year over year

53
00:01:52,370 --> 00:01:53,850
Data center revenue for the quarter

54
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:56,030
hit $107.6 billion

55
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:58,280
and Q4 guidance was about $130 billion

56
00:01:58,730 --> 00:01:59,810
Not a single order was lost

57
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:01,220
But on the same earnings call

58
00:02:01,270 --> 00:02:03,570
the chairman said they had no intention of reinstating him

59
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:06,930
and set up a new export control oversight committee

60
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:09,170
So what were they actually afraid of

61
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:10,530
Not performance

62
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:13,220
but keeping everything at the company compliant

63
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:16,560
Performance can prove the company keeps running

64
00:02:17,090 --> 00:02:18,790
The stock price and the petition prove

65
00:02:19,190 --> 00:02:21,030
that with him gone, people don't believe in it

66
00:02:21,610 --> 00:02:23,110
Both things are true at the same time

67
00:02:24,030 --> 00:02:26,310
From an accounting point of view

68
00:02:26,670 --> 00:02:29,320
a company's assets are its plants, equipment

69
00:02:29,370 --> 00:02:31,810
patents, cash, and goodwill

70
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:34,450
So do people go on the balance sheet

71
00:02:35,070 --> 00:02:35,770
They don't

72
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,530
But the market does count them

73
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:38,760
On November 23

74
00:02:38,810 --> 00:02:41,430
the board unanimously voted to reinstate him

75
00:02:41,870 --> 00:02:44,950
As soon as the news broke, the stock opened up about 30%

76
00:02:45,510 --> 00:02:46,740
That wasn't for the earnings

77
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:48,440
it was a welcome-back gift for him

78
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:51,380
Economics has a term for this: key-person risk

79
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:54,450
when a company's value depends too heavily on one person

80
00:02:54,970 --> 00:02:56,050
In plain terms

81
00:02:56,650 --> 00:02:58,670
that person's judgment, relationships, and reputation

82
00:02:59,110 --> 00:03:00,890
have become part of the company's assets

83
00:03:01,450 --> 00:03:03,250
I don't understand this area very deeply

84
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:04,380
so if I get anything wrong

85
00:03:04,430 --> 00:03:06,550
please point it out in the comments

86
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:07,830
and we'll learn together

87
00:03:08,370 --> 00:03:10,060
I had AI do some research first

88
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:12,560
going through the announcements and reports from these two weeks

89
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:15,160
and dug up an old case: Steve Jobs

90
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:17,710
He left Apple in 1985

91
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:19,150
and didn't come back until '97

92
00:03:19,630 --> 00:03:20,670
12 years in between

93
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:23,440
Apple's market share fell to around 3%

94
00:03:23,970 --> 00:03:26,500
In 1996 alone, it lost $1.5 billion

95
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:28,480
Every week, the market was guessing

96
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:30,240
who was going to buy it

97
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:31,740
The person was the same person

98
00:03:32,150 --> 00:03:33,320
the company was the same company

99
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:35,440
The difference was never the product

100
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:37,580
it was who was making the calls

101
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:39,900
It's the same with Nvidia. Over the past 33 years

102
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:41,520
how to build products for China

103
00:03:41,950 --> 00:03:43,300
how to deal with regulators

104
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:45,240
which customers get supply first

105
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:48,640
many decisions relied on his personal judgment and network

106
00:03:49,150 --> 00:03:51,300
None of that shows up in the financial statements

107
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:54,140
but the market prices it every single day

108
00:03:54,190 --> 00:03:56,090
It reminds me of a startup course at Peking University

109
00:03:56,140 --> 00:03:57,540
where a teacher talked about judging people

110
00:03:57,960 --> 00:03:59,370
He said the most expensive mistake early in a startup

111
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:01,660
isn't raising money at a low valuation

112
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:03,900
it's putting the wrong person in a key position

113
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:07,030
Core talent matters more fundamentally than financial leverage

114
00:04:07,610 --> 00:04:09,560
because people decide where the money goes

115
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:11,930
I agreed with that wholeheartedly

116
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:13,160
So my takeaway is this

117
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,640
a company's most valuable asset

118
00:04:15,690 --> 00:04:17,660
is sometimes just one person's judgment

119
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:20,120
It never shows up on the balance sheet

120
00:04:20,590 --> 00:04:22,960
but you can see traces of it in the stock price

121
00:04:23,930 --> 00:04:25,530
So let's flip the question

122
00:04:25,890 --> 00:04:27,340
If a company can't do without one person

123
00:04:27,390 --> 00:04:30,370
is that good or bad for shareholders

124
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:32,660
The good news: that person's judgment is still there

125
00:04:32,710 --> 00:04:34,290
so the company still has its soul

126
00:04:34,770 --> 00:04:36,520
The bad news: he'll leave eventually

127
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:39,630
And this time it was an investigation, a resignation, then a return

128
00:04:40,150 --> 00:04:42,430
which shows the system can't run on its own

129
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:44,900
The board first said no, then asked him back

130
00:04:44,950 --> 00:04:46,650
with only six days in between

131
00:04:47,250 --> 00:04:48,090
But the way I see it

132
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:50,680
this wasn't a purely business decision

133
00:04:51,130 --> 00:04:53,330
it was governance giving way to one person

134
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:55,140
Earlier I mentioned what the Peking University teacher said

135
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:58,240
putting the wrong person in a key position is the costliest call

136
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:00,500
Nvidia is the reverse case

137
00:05:00,550 --> 00:05:02,120
The right person is in the key position

138
00:05:02,540 --> 00:05:03,600
and the company can't do without him

139
00:05:03,970 --> 00:05:05,660
That's a potentially fatal risk too

140
00:05:06,020 --> 00:05:06,860
For ordinary people

141
00:05:07,390 --> 00:05:08,790
that person at your company who, once they leave

142
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:10,720
brings the whole company to a halt

143
00:05:11,220 --> 00:05:13,240
is an asset, and also the biggest risk

144
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:14,500
And for you

145
00:05:15,030 --> 00:05:16,980
becoming that person is your security

146
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:18,450
and also your ceiling

147
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:19,550
As for investing

148
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,190
when you buy a company, besides its financials

149
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:22,960
and its business

150
00:05:23,350 --> 00:05:25,690
you also need to look at what happens

151
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:26,880
when its decision-maker leaves

152
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:29,020
Succession plans, governance structure

153
00:05:29,490 --> 00:05:30,700
how much it depends on key people

154
00:05:31,090 --> 00:05:32,260
are all things to weigh in the decision

155
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:35,640
Nvidia proved this time that it can't do without its founder

156
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:37,910
and also that it still hasn't found

157
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:39,320
a better successor

158
00:05:39,370 --> 00:05:40,450
A real moat is

159
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:42,760
when the project, the company, keeps running no matter who leaves

160
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:44,460
A real premium is

161
00:05:44,910 --> 00:05:46,700
when losing one person turns it into something else

162
00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:48,810
Both being true at once

163
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:49,720
is the hardest thing of all

164
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:52,420
If you are the person at your company who can't leave

165
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:53,820
is that a good thing

166
00:05:54,250 --> 00:05:56,300
or something you should start planning for early

167
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:58,340
I'll leave that question for all of you
